# ============================================================================
# orca-serve.conf.template — SHARED HOST configuration (global, all instances inherit)
#
# Deployed to @PREFIX@/etc/orca-serve.conf = /data/opt/revive/orca_serve/etc/orca-serve.conf
# and loaded by orca-serve@.service as the FIRST EnvironmentFile= line (before the
# per-instance instances/<slot>.env, which may override any value here).
#
# Authoring rule (CONTRACT.md v2 §2): a value goes HERE if it could ever differ by HOST.
# A value that differs by INSTANCE or VERSION goes in etc/instances/<slot>.env instead.
# Never duplicate a value in both places. Every value below is config, not script data —
# editing + redeploying this file must never require a script or unit rebuild.
#
# Copy to orca-serve.conf (drop the .template suffix) and set per-host values.
# ============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host identity
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which deployment environment this host is. Used by lifecycle scripts for
# logging/labeling AND is the Orca environment id clients must resolve against.
# Must exactly match a `name` in the environment registry the serve binds.
#   mtl-02-dev-001  -> mtl-02
#   lesleys-pc      -> pc
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ORCA_ENVIRONMENT=mtl-02

# The address clients pair to on this host (overlay/WireGuard IP). Remote clients
# dial ws://<this>:6768 (or wss:// when proxied). Local/loopback hosts set 127.0.0.1.
ORCA_PAIRING_ADDRESS=10.200.0.1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Display strategy — the ONE knob that selects Xvfb wiring (CONTRACT §2a / DDR #3).
#   native  -> Orca self-spawns its own Xvfb on :99 (DISPLAY unset) + the inlined
#              stale-:99-lock guard drop-in. SERVER default.
#   managed -> a systemd-owned orca-xvfb.service on :99 + a DISPLAY=:99 drop-in.
#              WSL/PC default (native self-spawn SIGSEGVs there).
# Flip this value and re-run install.sh; the installer rewires the display with ZERO
# script edits.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ORCA_DISPLAY_MODE=native

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GPU / hardware policy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Force software GL for GPU-less hosts. Missing on a GPU-less host => WebGL2
# blocklisted => SIGTRAP / renderer crash (Bucket 1 in the matrix).
LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1

# Policy: Chromium sandbox. Set 1 only where the headless runtime cannot sandbox
# (documented host constraint); otherwise leave UNSET to keep the sandbox.
# ELECTRON_DISABLE_SANDBOX=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logging / diagnostics verbosity (HOST-wide default)
#
# There is exactly ONE verbosity knob for the serve: ELECTRON_ENABLE_LOGGING.
# The runtime has NO app-level log-level facility. `ORCA_LOG_LEVEL` and `ORCA_DEBUG`
# are not implemented anywhere in the Orca source, and `orca serve` has no
# `--verbose` flag — its whole allowlist is help/json/pairing-code/environment/
# port/pairing-address/mobile-pairing/no-pairing/project-root/recipe-json, and any
# other flag is rejected as "Unknown flag --x for command: serve". Do not
# reintroduce those three from an old runbook; see
# docs/guides/orca-serve-logging-guide.md §2 for the real, evidence-backed levers.
#
# A slot may override the value below in instances/<slot>.env (that file is the
# SECOND EnvironmentFile=, so it wins) — e.g. canary logging while factory stays quiet.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Turn on Electron/Chromium internal logging (GPU, renderer, IPC channels) to the
# served stderr -> journald + serve-fg.log. Leave UNSET/0 in steady state; enable
# only while chasing a WebGL/GPU/native-crash fault.
ELECTRON_ENABLE_LOGGING=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Any future host-wide tunable goes here — never in a script or in orca-serve@.service.
#
# DO NOT put per-user agent hub paths here (CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR / GROK_HOME /
# GH_CONFIG_DIR / …). Serve is shared infrastructure; per-user agent auth belongs in
# instances/<slot>.env or the client-side .auth/<user> wrappers.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------